{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nPlan review emits one defect as several BLOCKING findings, one per criterion that touches it\n(evidence in this ticket's comments: 164 emitted findings for 137 distinct issues across 11\njudged bakeoff runs, 24 judge-confirmed duplicate groups, 28 duplicate members carrying\n`decision=block`). Plan authors therefore see the same blocking defect counted several times.\n\nMain already has a stamp-only grouping seam for blocking findings,\n`_group_blocking_fix_units` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/floors.py:197`, called at\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/__init__.py:526` after the floors and cross-checks, before\n`_finalize_signature`), which stamps `group_id`, `is_primary`, `group_criteria`, and a CLI\nrenderer that collapses a group to its primary (`src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py:486-503`).\nIts key, `sidecar.fix_unit_key` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sidecar.py:321`), needs identical\ntoken sets and has never matched: 0 of 9,756 local `REVIEW_RESULT` sidecars carry a stamp.\n\nOperator decision 2026-11-02 (option 18, recorded in this ticket's comments): replace the\nlexical key with ONE set-level grouping LLM call over the BLOCKING findings.\n\n## Approach\n\nOne structured sub-call, modelled on the bakeoff's step-B within-arm grouping\n(`~/plan-review-bakeoff/LINKAGE.md` sections 2-3): a single call sees all blocking findings\nand returns the groups that state the same defect. No pairwise votes, no transitive\nchaining, and a finding belongs to at most one group. A set-level call fits this problem\nbecause the duplicates are rewordings across criteria that no lexical key catches (the\nmeasured lexical rules caught at most 5 of 10 duplicate blocking pairs), and pairwise\nlinking with connected components chains unrelated issues through compound findings\n(LINKAGE.md section 2).\n\nThe call follows the existing post-decide sub-call idiom in\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/xcheck.py` (`_assess_contradictions`, line 183): a library prompt\nfile with `category: plan-review-pass`, a pydantic structured-output contract registered via\n`contracts.register_contract`, a `RunRequest.for_structured(...)` request on the standard\nverifier class (`_verifier_cfg`), greedy sampling (`temperature=0.0`, as\n`floors._score_floor_novelty` does) so a re-review re-groups the same way, and fail-safe\nexception handling. It stays at the existing seam rather than becoming a\n`gates/plan-review.yaml` workflow step because the Pass-3 floors and cross-checks that run\nafter the workflow can still drop blocking findings; grouping after them means no group can\nname a dropped finding.\n\nBehaviour of `_group_blocking_fix_units(verdict, *, ctx, cfg, runner, repo_root)`:\n\n1. Fewer than 2 blocking findings, or blocking ids not unique: no call, no stamps.\n2. One call with each blocking finding listed by `id`, criteria, location and text.\n   Output contract `plan_review_fix_unit_groups`: `{\"groups\": [{\"defect\": str,\n   \"members\": [finding ids]}]}`.\n3. Validation, fail open: if the call raises, returns a non-list, names an id not in\n   the blocking set, or puts one id in two groups, emit NO groups and log a warning.\n   Groups with fewer than 2 distinct members are ignored.\n4. Stamping keeps the citric-alert-dogfish contract unchanged: each member of a valid group\n   gains `group_id` (`\"g\"` + first 16 hex digits of sha256 over its sorted member ids) and\n   `is_primary`; the primary (existing rule: highest priority, then alphabetically-first\n   sorted criterion, then lowest id; missing priority sorts as 0.0) gains `group_criteria`\n   (sorted criteria union). Every member keeps `decision=block` and stays in\n   `verdict[\"blocking\"]`; the verdict string, counts and buckets do not change.\n5. Accounting: `verdict[\"coverage\"][\"fix_unit_grouping\"]` records `status`\n   (`grouped` / `no_groups` / `failed`), `groups` (count) and the call's `_usage`.\n   The runner's existing usage log and step-failure tally cover the call like any\n   other sub-call. When no call is made, coverage is untouched.\n\nAdvisory findings stay out of scope.\n\n`sidecar.fix_unit_key` itself is NOT deleted: the criterion-effectiveness harness uses it as\nits cross-round match key (`evals/plan-review-gate/harnesses/criterion_effectiveness.py:271`).\nOnly its use as the grouping key is removed, with no lexical fallback.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/floors.py` — replace the lexical grouping with the sub-call,\n  its contract model and registration, validation and stamping.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/__init__.py` — pass `ctx`, `cfg`, `runner`, `repo_root` at the\n  call site.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/passes.py` — add the `PASS_FIX_UNIT_GROUPING` prompt id beside\n  `PASS_COMMENT_TRAIL` (line 153).\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_fix_unit_grouping.md` — new prompt file.\n- `tests/unit/test_blocking_fix_unit_grouping.py` — rewrite the grouping tests on\n  `FakeRunner`; keep the `fix_unit_key` unit tests.\n- `tests/unit/test_subcall_class_selection.py` — add the new `RunRequest` site to the\n  census (`_EXPECTED_RUN_REQUEST_SITES`, count 20 to 21).\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md` — one paragraph on blocking fix-unit grouping.\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tests with `rebar.llm.runner.FakeRunner(structured=...)` and a recording runner:\n\n- happy path: a returned group stamps `group_id`/`is_primary`/`group_criteria`, all members\n  remain in `blocking` with `decision=block`, verdict string unchanged;\n- 0 or 1 blocking findings: the runner is never called;\n- fail open: runner raises; output names an unknown id; one id in two groups; malformed\n  `groups` — each leaves no stamps and an unchanged verdict;\n- primary tie-breaks (existing rule), sidecar persistence via `build_payload`, CLI collapse.\n\nOffline eval (not a CI test): run the production prompt through the real runner\n(`AWS_PROFILE=frontier-bedrock`) over each bakeoff run's blocking findings for the 8 runs\nholding the 24 judge-confirmed duplicate groups, and score recall on judged duplicate\nblocking pairs and false merges on other blocking pairs.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_blocking_fix_unit_grouping.py` passes, including tests that\n      (a) stamp a group from FakeRunner output, (b) make no runner call with fewer than 2\n      blocking findings, (c) emit no stamps for a raising runner, an unknown id, an id in\n      two groups, and malformed output, and (d) assert the verdict string and the blocking\n      list length are unchanged in every case.\n- [ ] `git grep -nF 'fix_unit_key' -- src/rebar/llm/plan_review/floors.py` returns no\n      lines (the lexical grouping is gone), and `sidecar.fix_unit_key` still exists for the\n      eval harness.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_subcall_class_selection.py tests/unit/test_plan_review.py\n      tests/unit/test_plan_review_sidecar.py` passes.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` exit 0 (module-size, complexity and mechanism\n      ratchets included).\n- [ ] The offline eval's recall and false-merge counts are recorded as a comment on this\n      ticket (no financial figures).\n- [ ] The Gerrit change carries `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1` and is merged."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793656863275287001,"uuid":"39692082-b3e1-47e1-a978-3f13b8f2cb68"}